Q: deduction of nontype template parameter std::array A: <p>For me this example looks over-complicated. This template parameter <code>p</code> and <code>pieces</code> seems overengineered. Just pass all sizes immediately without explicitly specifying expected number of required sizes.</p>
<p>Can you explain why this simplification doesn't meet your requirements:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;cstdint&gt;

template &lt;std::size_t... Sizes&gt;
struct A {
    A(std::array&lt;unsigned int, Sizes&gt;...) { }
};

int main()
{
    A&lt;2, 3&gt; a({ 1, 3 }, { 1, 2, 5 });

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/TqMfd5fMq" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/TqMfd5fMq</a></p>
<p>Are you exacting this arrays passed to a constructor can hold different types of elements?</p>

Q: How to write UT for non-virtual functions? A: <p>There is a confusion here. To test a function you don't mock it. You mock other parts that would otherwise make it difficult to set up a unit test for the function you want to test.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>int foo(bar&amp; b, int y) {
    // do something
    int x = b.f(y);
    // do something with x
    return x;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to test <code>foo</code> independent of the actual implementation of <code>bar</code>, and if <code>bar::f</code> is virtual, you can write a mock for <code>bar</code> that overrides <code>f</code> and produces well defined output. If the actual implementation of <code>bar</code> has a bug, that will not disturb the test of <code>foo</code>, but only a separate test of <code>bar::f</code>.</p>
<p>Because of that, your question is difficult to comprehend. Taken literally:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How to write UT for non-virtual functions?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Same as for virtual functions.</p>
<p>Perhaps what copilot told you was along the line of: To test <code>foo</code> you can/should mock <code>bar::f</code> and thats easily possible if <code>bar::f</code> is virtual. I doubt copilot told you to make the function under test virtual, because I don't see what advantage that would bring.</p>
<p>It is advisable to write code such that it is testable. And if your production code is not testable, you should consider to refactor it so it is testable. &quot;But Production code cant be changed&quot; and &quot;I need to cover a failed case&quot; is a contradiciton anyhow. If production code has a bug, you have to change it.</p>
<p>A concreate example would help to help you more.</p>
